I don't think so.  It just seems that message gets triggered if you
teleport a shopkeeper out of his shop, thereby angering him, then pick
up or use up something from his shop and try to pay for it while he's
still outside his shop and you're still inside.

The code seems pretty weirdly organized.  You'd think it would check
for the obvious cases (shopkeeper robbed, shopkeeper angry, you have
unpaid items or debit to pay) in order, but no: it first checks for
wandering non-angry shopkeepers (who it assumes might be robbed) and
then for shopkeepers you have no unpaid items or debit from (who it
assumes might be angry and/or robbed, with some redundancy to the
first case).  If both of those checks fail (i.e. shopkeeper is angry
or in his shop, and you have unpaid items or debit), it assumes you're
making a normal in-shop purchase and does a final consistency check to
ensure that both you and the shopkeeper are in his shop.  That's the
check whose failure produces the message you saw.

Anyway, it's a rare corner case, and should be easy to circumvent:
just let him step back into his shop, step out of the shop yourself
(thereby robbing the items) or drop the unpaid items you're carrying.

(Note: All of this is from looking at the source -- I haven't actually
tested this, so I might have made a mistake somehere.  But I do feel
pretty confident in saying that the message you got should be harmless
and isn't likely to actually mean your game is messed up.)

